        <paragraphs>
            <title>Writing in MTH 210</title>
            <p>
                Writing habits, processes, and skills will regularly be taught when introducing a new proof technique.
                As part of writing, we will learn to type in the mathematical typesetting software LaTeX (via Overleaf).
                The main type of writing assignment in this course will be proofs.
                On the proof portfolio, you will have opportunities to revise each proof for the following week, and then revise again for the final portfolio.
                You will receive detailed feedback on both the handwritten draft and on the typed revision, so you will get feedback on your writing weekly.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Math Center</title>
           <p> The math center is <em>not</em> to be used for this course. </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-SOR">
        <title>Course Objectives (Syllabus of Record)</title>

        <p>
            <term>Learning Objectives:</term> After successful completion of the
            course, students will be able to:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Explain standard methods of mathematical proof
                        (including direct proofs, logical equivalencies, proof
                        by contradiction, mathematical induction, case analysis,
                        and counterexamples).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Create mathematical proofs using standard methods of
                        mathematical proof.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Solve and generalize mathematical problems.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Critique mathematical proofs.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Determine appropriate method of proof for a given
                        conjecture or argument.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- <subsection xml:id="subsec-other">
        <title/>
        <p>
            Learning Targets and Quizzes Goals: There are a number of basic
            skills that you should demonstrate fluency with in an introduction
            to proofs course.
            These quizzes are intended to emphasize a few things.
            One is that learning is a process.
            You will not be penalized for not demonstrating fluency with a skill
            the first time, but you are expected to eventually get there.
            Another is that fluency with basic skills and knowing definitions is
            an important part of doing mathematics.
            For example, you shouldn't expect to be able to complete a complex
            proof about the notion of divides if you don't know the definition
            of divides.
            These quizzes also provide individual assessment so you know whether
            or not you are keeping up with course material.
            Structure: We will have a closed book/closed note checkpoint each
            Tuesday at the end of class assessing the following learning
            targets.
            While each target will be graded as only either ``S" for
            ``Satisfactory" or ``NY" for ``does Not Yet meet the standard" each
            target appears on 3 consecutive quizzes.
            Once you have earned an “S” on a target you do not need to do so
            again.
            Blackboard will be updated weekly to reflect your “S”s and “NY”s.
            You will be notified which targets will be on a checkpoint, but it
            is your responsibility to know which targets you have yet to earn
            satisfactory.
            You may bring a 3x5 notecard with notes for each checkpoint.
            Each target will be on three quizzes.
            Your grade for this component will be the percentage of the 20
            targets listed below that you have earned an S on.
            Learning Targets: Come to office hours to ask a question or attend a
            math event.
            (See the math major emails for available math events, and let me
            know if you don't get it!) State precisely the definition of an even
            and odd integer and outline the proof of a statement using these
            terms.
            Given a statement to be proven using a direct proof, identify the
            assumptions to be made and the statements to be proven.
            Evaluate if a given proof is valid and adheres to our writing
            guidelines.
            Create and translate sets using set builder notation and fluently
            work with sets written in set builder notation.
            Identify if a given set of numbers is closed under a given
            operation.
            Use the symbols related to sets (like ∈, ∉, ⊆, ⊂, =, ⊈) correctly.
            Given two sets and a universal set identify the union, intersection,
            complement, and set difference and find the power set of a given
            set.
            State the definition of ``a divides b" and ``a is congruent to b
            modulo n", and correctly apply these definitions in examples.
            Identify situations in which it is appropriate to use induction and
            state the procedure for proving a statement by induction.
            Clearly and correctly disprove a statement using a counterexample.
            Construct truth tables for statements that use the logical operators
            and, or, not, and implies, and make conclusions about the truth
            values of these statements.
            Given a conditional statement, identify the hypothesis and
            conclusion, determine its truth value, and apply it.
            Negate a statement with and, or, not, implies, exists, and/or for
            all.
            Given a statement to be proven using a proof by contrapositive,
            identify the assumptions to be made and the statements to be proven.
            Given a statement to be proven using a proof by contradiction,
            identify the assumptions to be made and the statements to be proven.
            Correctly use function terminology such as domain, codomain, range,
            dependent variable, independent variable, image, and preimage.
            State the definition of injection, surjection, and bijection, create
            examples of each, and identify if a given function is an injection,
            surjection, or bijection.
            Prove or disprove that a given relation is reflexive, symmetric,
            and/or transitive and identify if the relation is an equivalence
            relation.
            Given an equivalence relation, I can determine the equivalence class
            of an element.
            I can list the distinct equivalence classes determined by the
            relation.
            Proof Portfolio At the end of the semester, you will submit a proof
            portfolio containing at least 10 proofs.
            Purpose There are a number of reasons why this exercise is important
            in an introduction to proofs class: Written communication.
            Perhaps one of the most important goals of the proof portfolio is to
            work on your ability to write well.
            I don't know anyone who was born a good mathematical writer.
            Writing mathematics is decidedly different from writing an essay
            (just as, you may have noticed, reading a math text is different
            from reading a novel) and it will take some time to get good at it.
            The proof portfolio gives you many opportunities to learn from
            mistakes.
            Learning LaTeX.
            Mathematical typesetting in LaTeX is an important goal in this
            course.
            In future courses you'll be able to do your homework faster and
            better, and it will look more professional.
            You'll be glad you learned it, trust me!
            The opportunity to act on feedback.
            You will get lots of feedback on assignments in this course,
            especially on proof drafts.
            This is a chance for you and me to make sure you understand my
            feedback and correct mistakes.
            Seeing a proof through to perfection and celebrating your progress.
            I hope this will be satisfying!
            A resource later.
            You will have 10 typed proofs and at least one example of every
            proof technique we've learned in the course.
            This could be of use in the event that you ever forget, say, how
            induction works.
            Collaboration and Resources You may use your textbook and class
            notes.
            You may brainstorm and discuss solutions in groups, but you may not
            take any notes from these discussions in any form (including video
            recording).
            You must write your solutions individually.
            You may only discuss your ideas with others in our section of MTH
            210 who are at the same point in solving the problem, as well as me.
            See academic integrity for more information and examples,
            particularly the golden rule.
            Drafts Drafts and deadlines: Each proof will have two deadlines
            during the semester: First a rough draft, then a revised copy that
            focuses on writing.
            Each is due Friday at 5:00 pm on Blackboard with a 48 hour grace
            period.
            Exact due dates posted on Blackboard and announced in class.
            Estimated due dates are in the schedule.
            Rough draft: First, complete a rough draft of your portfolio
            solution.
            This could be in “know-show table” format or bullet points.
            Your goal is to include all key mathematical ideas and work, with
            just enough writing and detail to make it understandable.
            Focus on the central ideas and don't try to make it beautiful (yet)!
            Due: Friday at 5:00 pm (plus a 48 hour grace period).
            Writing revision: I'll give feedback on your rough draft very
            quickly, focusing only on the mathematical ideas.
            Spend the next week revising it and bringing the writing up to meet
            the professional communication specifications.
            This writing revision is due the following Friday at 5:00 pm (plus a
            48 hour grace period).
            Here's an example of a well done rough draft and writing revision.
            How to submit your work: Upload to the corresponding assignment on
            Blackboard.
            Upload a PDF of your work.
            Submit writing revisions to the same assignment.
            For the writing revision, you may want to Include a “read only” link
            to your Overleaf document in the comments field so I can help with
            LaTeX Grades on drafts: The rough draft will not receive a grade.
            Blackboard insists I give one, so I will enter a 0.
            You did NOT get a 0 on the assignment.
            For the written revision, I will assign a grade according to this
            Proof Portfolio Rubric.
            This grade is not final, you may continue to revise your work, see
            final grading.
            Final Product Final product: You will put together all of your final
            proofs into one Overleaf file.
            Here's a template.
            (See Blackboard for how to get an Overleaf file of your own.) In
            addition to the problems, the final product will contain a short
            reflection.
            The reflection should be a paragraph (say 4-5 sentences) and include
            an answer to what what you learned from doing (or redoing) a
            problem.
            Here are some questions to guide your reflection: did you learn a
            particular strategy?
            Did you make a mistake and what did you learn from it?
            What vocabulary was important to know to do the problem?
            What was a key insight you had to have to do the problem?
            Was this problem particularly challenging for you?
            If so, why?
            what portfolio objectives the problem addresses (along with a
            justification).
            See proof portfolio objectives for the list.
            Final deadline: The whole portfolio is due, with reflections and
            objectives at the start of our final exam time (Tuesday, April 28 at
            10:00AM).
            Final grading: These final submissions are the only ones whose
            grades count.
            Everything up until then is feedback to help you write the best
            possible proofs.
            After feedback on the writing revision, you can continue to revise
            as much as you would like.
            I'm happy to meet with you during drop-in hours to talk about
            subsequent revisions.
            In the Overleaf template for the final portfolio there is a table to
            fill out where you will assign yourself a grade for each of the
            problems, justifying if your grade is different from the one on the
            writing revision, as well as a grade for reflections and overall
            meeting of the portfolio objectives.
            Here are the point values: Problems/proofs (100 points): You will
            complete 10 problems/proofs, 10 points each.
            Reflections (10 points): Each reflection is worth 1 point.
            Assign yourself 1 point for a well thought out reflection, half a
            point if you started a reflection, and 0 points if you didn't do a
            reflection for that problem.
            Portfolio Objectives (20 points): Assign yourself a grade out of 20
            points based on how many of the portfolio objectives you met.
            The final portfolio is out of 130 points.
            Proof Portfolio Objectives Proof methods: PM1 Write a correct direct
            proof.
            PM2 Write a correct proof using the contrapositive or other logical
            equivalence.
            PM3 Write a correct proof by contradiction.
            PM4 Use cases correctly in a proof.
            PM5 Write a correct proof using mathematical induction.
            PM6 Correctly prove a biconditional statement in which both
            directions are true.
            PM7 Clearly and correctly disprove a statement using a
            counterexample.
            PM8 Correctly prove a set relation using element-chasing or set
            algebra.
            PM9 Correctly prove that a function is injective, surjective, or
            bijective.
            PM10 Correctly cite a previously proved theorem in the proof of
            another theorem.
            Content: Co1 Correctly use the idea of closure.
            Co2 Correctly use the definitions of odd and even or divisibility.
            Co3 Correctly use the definitions of rational and irrational.
            Co4 Correctly use congruences, modular arithmetic, and modular
            arithmetic shortcuts.
            Co5 Fluently work with set builder notation and an arbitrary element
            from a set.
            Co6 Correctly use function terminology (domain, codomain, range,
            image, and preimage).
            Co7 Correctly use the definition of equivalence relation and its
            parts (symmetric, reflexive, transitive).
            Mathematical Strategies MS1 Correctly use appropriate mathematical
            ideas from previous classes (such as Algebra and Calculus) to solve
            problems.
            MS2 Create a variety of concrete examples that illustrate the use of
            a theorem.
            MS3 Create a variety of concrete examples that illustrate the use of
            a definition.
            Proof Portfolio Rubric Score Characteristics 10 (Distinguished) A 10
            is reserved for completely correct and well written proofs or
            solutions.
            Includes: Clearly communicated understanding of key ideas
            Explanations and justifications are complete, correct, and easy to
            follow.
            Mathematical terminology and notation are used correctly.
            Reasoning flows logically.
            Computations are essentially correct (could contain one or two minor
            errors that do not detract from the solution), well organized, and
            easy to follow.
            Adheres to all writing guidelines.
            8 (Proficient) For an 8 the proof or solution has the main flow of
            logic correct, but misses one of the high standards of a 10
            Communication of key ideas is essentially correct but may contain
            minor flaws that do not subtract significantly from the solution.
            Explanations and justifications are essentially complete, correct,
            and can be followed, but may contain minor errors that subtract, but
            not significantly, from the solution.
            Mathematical terminology and notation are used at least mostly
            correctly.
            Reasoning flows logically.
            Computations are essentially correct, but may contain minor errors
            that subtract, but not significantly, from the solution.
            Adheres to at least most of the writing guidelines.
            6 (Progressing significantly) A 6 is earned for proofs or solutions
            that show significant progress towards a solution, but have a key
            error.
            Communication contains many of the key ideas but also may contain
            errors that subtract significantly from the solution.
            Explanations and justifications exist, but are not always clear and
            easy to follow or they do not match the work done.
            Mathematical terminology and notation are at least sometimes used
            correctly.
            Work done has some logical foundation, but flow of ideas may not
            easily be followed.
            A computation may contain an error that subtracts significantly from
            the solution.
            Adheres to at least some of the writing guidelines.
            3 (Progressing) A 3 is earned for proofs or solutions that have the
            basics, but have a major flaw that make it invalid.
            Communication demonstrates understanding of some key ideas, but also
            demonstrates a lack of understanding of other ideas.
            Some claims have sufficient explanations or justifications, but some
            are missing entirely or are hard to follow.
            There may be a significant logical error.
            The solution is disorganized or contains irrelevant information or
            arguments.
            Mathematical terminology or notation may be nonsensical or
            incorrectly used.
            Adheres to at least a few of the writing guidelines.
            0 (Inadequate) A 0 is given to a proof or solution that is invalid
            and demonstrates no understanding or is work that is not your own.
            The reasoning is logically invalid.
            Claims are not justified or the explanations are incomprehensible.
            In addition to these grades, I may assign a “9.9”.
            A 9.9 means there are errors that should be fixed, but I believe
            that you can fix them on your own.
            Policies Specific to this Course 
            This course is subject to the GVSU policies listed at
            http://www.gvsu.edu/coursepolicies/.
